            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Adia"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Took other reviewers suggestion and added 1.5 tbsp sugar and baked in oven for 7-9 minutes at 350. Perfect crust! Use this for a banana cream pie.",
            "datePublished": "2018-12-30T18:59:54.770Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Dani"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It was so good with one change! My mom, who usually makes pies from scratch at home, said it was excellent. It held well for cutting and serving. Good texture. Taking other reviewers suggestions, I used a food processor to crumble the cookies and then cut in the butter. Also baked the crust first for about 8 or 9 min at 350F and let it cool before adding filling. Excited to use this crust for all kinds of pies!",
            "datePublished": "2017-06-19T01:41:55.017Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "MarkA"
            },
            "reviewBody": "A great starter recipe that needs a lot of work. Keeping the story as short as I can.....40 cookies, 1/4 C butter, 1/3 C powdered sugar, a handful of walnuts, splash of vanilla. Pulsed in a processor. Took the advice of an earlier reviewer and baked at 350F for nine minutes. Poured in a Meyers Lemon filling and cooked for another 30 minutes. Maybe the best pie I've eaten and the crust was perfect. IMHO you have to cook this crust, especially with a non-bake pie, or the crust will be mush. I used a Marie Callender size pie tin and had more crust than I needed. Next time I'll use 30 cookies and maybe a tad more nuts....pecans or macadamias should be awesome in this crust. Anyway, thanks for a great idea.",
            "datePublished": "2012-04-16T18:10:01.953Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "mikeswy31"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It was good, however I would use 2 cups of crushed vanilla wafers for a 9in. pie crust",
            "datePublished": "2016-11-28T23:32:00.913Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "lissalea"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Good change from the normal graham cracker crust. I used my food processor, and pulsed the crumbs, butter, some orange zest and a little cinnamon sugar. I baked at 350 for 10 minutes, cooled, and filled with a pudding/cool whip mixture. Yum!",
            "datePublished": "2010-06-21T09:16:10.513Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Lucy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I also added sugar, 1 tsp brown and 1 tsp white plus 1/4 tsp salt. I took about 30 minutes to set up in the freezer.",
            "datePublished": "2024-11-15T10:46:26.682Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jacolyn"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was an easy crust (and especially good w/ the \"Banana Cream Pie I\" recipe from this site). Changes I made include: 1) I didn't measure the crumbs exactly - just approximated it and added more butter if it was a little dry. 2) I enlisted the help of my husband to crush the cookies in a Ziplock freezer bag w/ a small hammer from his toolbox over a cutting board (much easier than using a meat pallet). 3) I baked it at 350 degrees for approximately 10 minutes until it wasn't too soft. 4) I added 1/3 cup of white sugar before pressing it into my pie pan.",
            "datePublished": "2012-06-22T12:34:11.910Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "MTmom"
            },
            "reviewBody": "An excellent crust, especially for fruit pies! I would, however, strongly suggest that you bake it before you fill it. I baked it at 350 for about 9-10 minutes for a full 9x13 pan. It turned out beautifully!",
            "datePublished": "2016-04-13T18:23:49.210Z"
